# Absurdly Accurate Clock — Consumer Edition Requirements

## Purpose

AAC-CE is the consumer/appliance-oriented derivative of the original
Absurdly Accurate Clock.

It inherits the proven AAC GPS/PPS timing architecture, but is intended
to behave like an exceptionally good normal household clock rather than
a laboratory/development instrument.

The original AAC remains the reference/development platform.

---

## 1. Timing Architecture

The existing AAC timing model remains fundamental:

GPS UTC label + GPS PPS
        ↓
qualification / association
        ↓
authoritative UTC state
        ↓
consumer time policy
        ↓
presentation
        ↓
display backend

The CE display must never perform independent timekeeping.

Canonical internal time remains UTC.

GPS parsing, PPS processing, UTC/PPS association, qualification and
reacquisition logic should be inherited from the existing AAC unless
measured evidence requires a change.

---

## 2. MCU / Build Target

AAC-CE targets Raspberry Pi Pico 2 / RP2350 only. RP2350 is the selected
hardware for Dad's clock and the sole current PlatformIO target. Do not add
RP2040 compatibility layers, alternate toolchains, or build targets.

Platform-specific display transport and hardware support should remain outside
the clock timing core.

---

## 3. Time Quality States

CE should expose at least three meaningful time states:

### UNSYNCED

No authoritative UTC/PPS relationship has been established since boot.

The clock must not present an arbitrary or stale time as valid.

### LOCKED

UTC is currently qualified against GPS/PPS.

### HOLDOVER

The clock was previously LOCKED but GPS/PPS authority has subsequently
been lost.

During HOLDOVER, time continues advancing from the MCU's local timing
source, using the RP2350's crystal-derived local timing. HOLDOVER is permitted
for at most 24 hours from the last qualified GPS/PPS reference.

The display must make HOLDOVER distinguishable from LOCKED without
making the clock unusable.

Reacquisition of GPS/PPS should automatically return the clock to
LOCKED operation only after the existing GPS/PPS qualification rules succeed.

At 24-hour expiration, estimated time becomes invalid and the clock enters
UNSYNCED until qualified GPS/PPS returns. A reset or power interruption does
not preserve holdover without additional hardware. Never represent estimated
local timing as GPS/PPS-verified timing.

The 24-hour limit is intended to be a compile-time configuration constant,
defaulting to 24 hours for this build. Holdover accuracy depends on the Pico 2
oscillator and operating conditions; no guaranteed accuracy may be claimed
without measurement. The inherited AAC timing engine does not implement
holdover.

---

## 4. Civil Time

UTC remains canonical internally.

Civil-time conversion is a consumer policy/presentation function.

Production personalization is selected at compile time. A physical UI button
is not part of Dad's build. The selected production timezone has not yet been
specified; the current Stage 1 firmware defaults to UTC. The existing civil
conversion supports Eastern, Central, Mountain, Pacific, and UTC.

Initial supported zones may reuse the existing AAC US timezone logic:

- Eastern
- Central
- Mountain
- Pacific

DST shall be derived from the configured civil timezone and date.

Do not persist "EST" or "EDT" as a setting.

If a timezone is selected in a build, represent the region and derive the active
abbreviation (for example EST/EDT) at runtime.

The active abbreviation should be available to the presentation layer.

---

## 5. 12 / 24 Hour Display

CE shall support:

- 12-hour civil time
- 24-hour civil time

The production mode is a compile-time personalization choice; its selected
default remains to be specified.

Changing display format must not change canonical UTC.

---

## 6. Production Personalization and Development Builds

Production personalization direction:

- timezone and 12/24-hour format are build-time choices, not persistent user
  settings in the selected design.

Dedicated development builds will support simulated GPS/PPS input, display
testing, diagnostics, animations, and theme demonstrations. The Stage 2
simulated source must feed the same timing qualification engine as real GPS/PPS.
Do not mix those development behaviors into production configuration by default.
Compile-time profile/source infrastructure is a later stage and is not part
of the Stage 1 implementation.

Authoritative UTC must never be restored from persistent storage and falsely
treated as GPS-qualified after a cold boot.

---

## 7. Main Display

The primary display exists to show time clearly.

Primary design goal:

    room-readable HH:MM

or another layout appropriate to the selected hardware.

Seconds may appear on the primary display, but are not required there.

The primary display should remain visually legible. Dad's build uses one
WS2812B 8×32 RGB panel (256 matrix pixels). The logical framebuffer must be
independent of physical serpentine/order mapping.

Display hardware is build-specific and replaceable.

Dad's selected display is one WS2812B 8×32 RGB panel (256 matrix pixels). The
inherited character VFD implementation remains available as an optional
backend. Other display technologies may be added behind the same backend
boundary.

The selected physical display must not dictate the timing architecture.

---

## 8. Matrix, Status Pixels, and Visual Effects

The WS2812B matrix is the selected primary display. Up to three optional
additional WS2812 pixels may be chained after the matrix for status; their use
and meanings are not fixed. WS2812 GP8 is proposed but not implemented or
physically tested.

Animation style is not fixed. Fades, dissolves, decays, color transitions, and
rolling effects are candidates for early development-build testing. Fonts,
overlays, calendar information, and special-day themes may be explored in
development builds. Production effects, status hierarchy, and exact layouts
remain to be specified and must preserve clear time-quality indication.

The provisional system power target is 5 V / 2 A. Verify actual panel current,
brightness/current limits, thermal behavior, level shifting, and power margin
by measurement. This target is not a measured result.

One of the optional three additional chained pixels is designated for
timing-quality indication: LOCKED, HOLDOVER, or UNSYNCED. Colors, fades, other
effects, timing, and brightness remain display-development decisions. The
status pixel shares the WS2812 serial chain and adds no Pico GPIO. It must never
imply that live GPS PPS is present while showing HOLDOVER. No status-pixel code
is implemented in Stage 1.

The selected electrical direction uses a 74HCT541 5 V level shifter and an
approximately 330 Ω series resistor. Exact circuit/layout and panel operation
remain subject to review and hardware verification. Dad's selected build has
no physical UI button.

---

## 9. Display Architecture

Presentation should consume a coherent snapshot of clock state.

Conceptually:

ClockCore
    ↓
ConsumerPolicy
    ↓
PresentationState
    ↓
DisplayBackend
    ↓
PhysicalDisplay

Display code must not:

- qualify GPS
- associate UTC with PPS
- advance authoritative UTC
- decide whether the clock is locked
- implement independent holdover timekeeping

Backends are selected at compile time; no runtime plugin system is required.
The shared backend contract consumes a coherent presentation snapshot and does
not require all backends to support RGB framebuffers, individual pixels, or
animations. Display-specific mapping, rendering/effects, and physical transport
stay behind the selected backend. Stage 1 selects the inherited PD-2200 backend
so the existing VFD build remains functional; no WS2812, MAX7219, or TFT driver
is implemented in that stage. The inherited VFD operates in Noritake serial
command mode. For the verified PD-2200, command `0x0E` clears displayed
characters without resetting the cursor/write position; `0x0C` homes it. This
behavior must not be assumed for other Noritake-capable displays. Command
protocol, PD-2200-specific behavior, and the physical UART/RS-232 transport are
separate layers.

The backend architecture is intended to accommodate WS2812 RGB matrices,
MAX7219 monochrome matrices and seven-segment displays, SPI TFT LCDs,
character VFDs including the PD-2200, and other technologies added later. These
are future backend targets, not Stage 1 drivers.

### Future multi-display composition

AAC-CE must accommodate future configurations with multiple heterogeneous
display backends operating simultaneously. Different mechanisms may present
different parts of the same clock presentation: for example, a robotic arm for
hours, an LCD for minutes, an LED matrix for seconds, a VFD for date or other
information, and independent status indicators for clock quality. These are
possible configurations, not hardware selected for Dad's build.

The authoritative GPS/PPS timing engine remains independent of every display.
Multiple backends may consume the same coherent, read-only presentation state,
with responsibilities assigned by each mechanism's capabilities and needs.
Backends may have different update frequencies, refresh policies, response
times, and hardware capabilities. Slow or electromechanical mechanisms must
not block authoritative timekeeping or unrelated display operations; future
backends may operate asynchronously where needed. The shared presentation
model must not require every backend to support a framebuffer, pixels, RGB,
characters, or instantaneous updates. Physical behavior and transport remain
backend responsibilities, and display composition must never create another
authoritative source of time.

This is a future requirement, not current functionality. Stage 1 supports one
compile-time-selected backend through `display_backend::Selected`, which
remains correct for the current build. Multiple concurrent backends, display
responsibility routing, scheduling, and asynchronous operations are not
implemented. Dad's selected display remains one WS2812B 8×32 RGB panel.

Display failure must not corrupt clock state.

---

## 10. Consumer Behavior

AAC-CE should behave as a normal person expects a high-quality clock
to behave.

Examples:

- Once synchronized, temporary GPS loss should not make the clock stop
  displaying useful time.
- GPS/PPS reacquisition should require no user intervention.
- Production personalization is compile-time; persistent consumer settings
  are not part of the selected design.
- Normal operation should require no network connection.
- Technical status should be available without dominating the display.
- The clock should normally require no routine maintenance or
  consumables.

---

## 11. Networking

Networking is NOT a CE requirement.

Dad's selected build has no Wi-Fi or AAC-Bridge support. Stage 1 removes Bridge
SPI, publisher/transport, TIME_SYNC, Bridge pin reservations, initialization,
and active source dependencies from the CE repository.

CE must function fully as:

    GPS
      ↓
    Pico
      ↓
    Displays

No Internet connection is required for normal operation.

---

## 12. Watchdog / Failure Behavior

Preserve the AAC watchdog philosophy.

Missing GPS, missing PPS or display failure are degraded operating
conditions, not firmware crashes.

A watchdog reset invalidates previous timing authority and requires
normal GPS/PPS reacquisition.

---

## 13. Startup Behavior

On a cold boot:

1. Apply compile-time production personalization.
2. Initialize displays and hardware.
3. Begin GPS/PPS acquisition.
4. Remain UNSYNCED until the existing qualification rules establish
   authoritative UTC.
5. Enter LOCKED state.
6. HOLDOVER is required after loss of previously qualified authority, but is
   limited to 24 hours from the last qualified reference, then expires to
   UNSYNCED. It is not implemented in Stage 1. On return of GPS/PPS, repeat the
   existing qualification before LOCKED.

Do not manufacture apparently valid wall-clock time before first
synchronization.

---

## 14. Product Philosophy

AAC-CE should be:

- standalone
- highly accurate to human perception
- GPS referenced
- useful during temporary GPS loss
- non-cloud-dependent
- non-"smart" unless a particular build genuinely requires networking
- understandable without technical knowledge
- attractive enough to live permanently on a shelf or desk
- serviceable and understandable internally

The engineering may be sophisticated.

The user experience should not be.
